lture:** Converts may encounter restrictive practices in some Muslim-majority societies and conclude that these arise from particular political systems, patriarchal customs, or selective interpretations—not necessarily from Islam itself. Critics, including many Muslim feminists, may disagree about where that line should be drawn. None of this means concerns about women’s rights in some Muslim communities or states are imaginary. Forced veiling, unequal family laws, restrictions on education or work, and religious coercion are serious realities in particular places. But it does not follow that every Muslim woman experiences Islam as oppression, or that women who convert are simply unaware of those issues. Many make an informed choice while interpreting and practicing the faith in ways they see as empowering.
